course: dictionary
lesson: 2572
title: Delusional: believing what is not true
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Delusional is an adjective, so it describes a person or a belief. Its first meaning is serious: a delusional person firmly believes things that are not true, even when someone shows them clear proof. This often happens because of an illness, so the person needs care and kindness.
explain: The second meaning is lighter. In everyday talk, delusional means having very unrealistic hopes or ideas, like thinking your team will win every match this year. «You're delusional!» can be a joke between friends, but it is still a word to use carefully.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. «Her delusional thinking made her sure the teacher had hidden her bag.» The word thinking tells you it is about beliefs, and she is sure even though there is no proof, so the belief is not true. Ask yourself: does this belief match the facts?
explain: Be careful when you use it. Calling someone delusional just to tease them is unkind, especially when they may be unwell. Think about how the other person might feel before you say it.
fact: Delusional means believing something that is not true, even when shown clear proof.
fact: In medicine, delusions can be a symptom of illness, and the person needs care and kindness, not mockery.
fact: In everyday talk, «You're delusional!» can mean someone has very unrealistic hopes, and it is often said as a joke.
fact: «Delulu» is slang for delusional, meaning far from reality.
fact: «Completely delusional» means totally unrealistic, as in «You're completely delusional, innit!» where «innit» means «isn't it» or «right?».
exercise: «The man firmly believed he was a famous singer, even when everyone showed him proof that he was not.» Find and copy one word that tells us he would not change his mind. | singer | everyone | famous | *firmly
exercise: Maya says her team will win every match this year, even though they have lost every game. Her friend laughs, «You're delusional!» What does it mean in this joke? | Knowing every rule of the game | Having a loud voice | Being very kind to others | *Having very unrealistic hopes
exercise: «The doctor explained that his delusional beliefs were a symptom of an illness, so he needed care.» What does delusional mean here? | *Believing things that are not true, even with proof | Being very funny and clever | Feeling very tired after a long day | Always telling the truth
test: Which sentence uses delusional in its everyday, joking sense? | «The river flowed quickly past the bridge.» | *«Dad thinks he can run a marathon tomorrow, which is totally delusional.» | «We walked to the shop after school.» | «The doctor checked his heartbeat carefully.»
test: What is the kindest way to talk about someone who is delusional because of an illness? | Ignore them and never speak to them again | Tell everyone at school about it | *Show care and kindness, and do not make fun of them | Laugh at them so they feel better
exam: Which sentence uses delusional in the medical sense? | *«The patient firmly believed the nurses were spies, even after the doctor explained the facts.» | «The puppy wagged its tail.» | «The cake was delicious and sweet.» | «We walked to the shop after school.»
